<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Bangwaketse Chiefdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1750 CE – 1885 CE (approx.) · capital Kanye</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Tswana chiefdom centred on Kanye whose rulers helped secure British protection rather than Company rule for Botswana.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangwaketse">Bangwaketse</a> separated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakwena">Bakwena</a> in the 18th century and settled in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makaba-ii">Makaba II</a> in the late 18th and early 19th centuries they became one of the strongest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tswana-people">Tswana</a> states, until Makaba was killed in 1824 during the wars of that era.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chiefs ruled from a large town through ward heads and the kgotla, drawing wealth from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and skins with the Cape. After years of displacement, Gaseitsiwe re-established the chiefdom at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanye">Kanye</a>, a defensible hill town. Missionaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> were influential.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The chiefdom joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bechuanaland-protectorate">Bechuanaland Protectorate</a> in 1885. In 1895 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bathoen-i">Bathoen I</a> travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khama-iii">Khama III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebele-i">Sebele I</a> to oppose transfer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> was preserved. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangwaketse">Ngwaketse</a> is now a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1750 CE – 1885 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:94.53%;width:2.69%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1750 CE – 1885 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kanye</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Makaba II, Gaseitsiwe, Bathoen I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Tswana religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Setswana</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/botswana">Botswana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ngwaketse</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1818&amp;focus=bangwaketse-chiefdom" aria-label="Bangwaketse Chiefdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5352 3097 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bangwaketse Chiefdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5672 3353L5688 3341"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5688 3341h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5672 3353h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5672 3353h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kanye. <a href="/atlas?year=1818&amp;focus=bangwaketse-chiefdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

